<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iberia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Iberian Peninsula, Iberian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iberia, or the Iberian Peninsula, is the south-western peninsula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, comprising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal. It was a major arena for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> and later ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>. It was also the homeland of the Portuguese and Spanish overseas empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iberia.jpg" alt="Iberia" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques Descloitres, MODIS Rapid Response Team, NASA/GSFC · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Espa%C3%B1a_y_Portugal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Keeping forces in North Africa and <b>Iberia</b> at the same time severely strained the empire's finances, a standing burden that contributed materially to its eventual fragmentation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almoravid gold dinar was so trusted that Christian <b>Iberia</b> imitated it as the morabetino, the origin of the Castilian maravedí.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…Caliphate of Córdoba made al-Andalus the wealthiest and most cultured state of tenth-century western Europe, rivalling the Abbasids and Fatimids, and its mosque, palaces, libraries and science had a long influence, even though its sudden collapse in civil war after 1009 broke Muslim <b>Iberia</b> into rival taifa kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>From the fourth century, according to Armenian and Albanian sources, a branch of the Arsacid family, related to the royal houses of Armenia and <b>Iberia</b>, ruled the country.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the protection of the counts and their vassals, troubadour lyric became the first great vernacular poetry of medieval Europe, shaping the tradition of courtly love that passed to the trouvères of northern France, the Minnesänger of Germany and poets in Italy and <b>Iberia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…many Mudéjar Muslim peasants; Catalonia combined a seigneurial countryside with the trading city of Barcelona; Valencia was a fertile coastal plain where a Christian settler minority ruled a large Muslim population for centuries after the conquest; and Majorca was a busy port between <b>Iberia</b>, Italy and North Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
